---
/**
 * foodcraft / ServicesBL — "Kalinaw Coffee": THE MENU.
 *
 * ROLE. The design's SERVICES element (id="menu"), verbatim: a split head —
 * eyebrow + display h2 on the left, one paragraph on the right, both sitting on
 * the same bottom edge — over a THREE-CARD GRID. Each card is a white plate
 * inside a warm hairline: an italic rust numeral and a tracked-caps tag on one
 * baseline across the top, then the dish name, then its blurb, then a foot
 * pinned to the bottom of the card by `margin-top:auto` and separated by a
 * paler hairline, carrying a small detail line left and the price hard right.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Nothing below is re-derived. The 30px head
 * gap and 36px head margin, the 20px grid gutter, the 28px/26px card padding,
 * the 9px card gap, the 16px foot offset, the 19px numeral, the 24px/1.15 card
 * heading, the 14px/1.72 blurb, the 12px detail and the 16px/500 price are the
 * design's own numbers, mapped hex-for-token: #FFFFFF --card · #E9DFCF --line ·
 * #F2EADC --line-3 · 16px --r-lg · #B4541F --accent · #241A12 --ink · #6B5C4E
 * --ink-soft · #948373 --muted. No literal hex appears in this file (R1).
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS — every one is declared in components/editor/
 * genericContentSchema.ts's `services` group, so none is a dead hook:
 *   services.tag                — eyebrow (.kick)
 *   services.headline           — h2, set:html so an owner's <em> becomes the
 *                                 design's italic rust emphasis
 *   services.sub                — the head's right-hand paragraph
 *   services.items.${i}.title   — the card h3
 *   services.items.${i}.desc    — the card paragraph
 *   services.items.${i}.tag     — the tracked-caps tag, top right of the card
 *   services.items.${i}.note    — the small detail line in the card foot, left
 *   services.items.${i}.price   — the price in the card foot, right
 *
 * DERIVED, AND ONLY THIS. The card numeral ("01", "02", …) is built from the
 * DISPLAY index, is aria-hidden decoration and is stored nowhere — the design
 * computes it the same way (`String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')`). Every other glyph on
 * this section is a string the owner typed.
 *
 * NOTHING IS SUPPLIED BY THIS FILE (R2). The design's demo cards — "Espresso
 * bar", "Single-origin pour-over", "Beans to take home", "250g bag", ₱145,
 * "All day" — are the DESIGNER filling their own template. A drink nobody
 * sells is an invented drink and a price nobody quoted is an invented price, so
 * not one of them is a fallback here. The price is printed VERBATIM: never
 * converted, never prefixed with a currency symbol this file chose, never
 * totalled or compared. Every guard sits OUTSIDE its element, so an empty value
 * removes the ELEMENT and not just its text — no empty tag pill, no heading over
 * nothing, and no foot hairline drawn under a foot that carries neither a detail
 * line nor a price.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, DELIBERATELY:
 *   · No button, anywhere. The design's menu cards carry no link and the section
 *     carries no action — the page's actions live in the header, the hero and
 *     the CTA band. So services.ctaLabel / services.ctaHref are left to the
 *     templates that actually draw a per-card button; emitting them here would
 *     put a live sidebar input on a control this page does not have.
 *   · NO ANCHOR HELPER (R7). Because the section draws no link at all, a copy of
 *     the sibling's onPage() remap would be unreachable code, and inventing a
 *     button to give it a job is the exact trade the family rule forbids. If a
 *     link is ever added here, copy onPage() from ServicesBK with
 *     PAGE_ANCHORS = {menu, about, gallery, location, faq, main} and
 *     ANCHOR_ALIAS = {visit/contact → location, services/rooms → menu,
 *     host/why → about, work → gallery, book → main}.
 *   · No card image, rating, meta line, unit or feature chips. The design's card
 *     is numeral / tag / title / blurb / detail / price and nothing else. Those
 *     schema fields belong to the zig-zag and card templates; a data-field for
 *     something this section never draws is an input that changes nothing.
 *   · No star anywhere. This section prints no rating, so it makes no rating
 *     claim. (Family rule: N stars are drawn only from a numeric rating field,
 *     clamped 1–5, aria-hidden with the number itself announced — never five
 *     stars over a review that carried none.)
 *   · services.headline is the only set:html field. The card h3 is plain text,
 *     as the design prints it — and a set:html child could not be reached by
 *     this scoped block anyway (R3).
 *
 * THE CARD FILTER. A card counts as real when it carries a title, a desc, a tag,
 * a note OR a price — the five things this card can draw. All five are counted
 * on purpose: each is a string an owner typed into a live input, so dropping a
 * card that holds only a price would silently delete their number. A row holding
 * none of them is an empty "+ Add" row, and it would spend a numeral, a hairline
 * and a whole plate on nothing visible.
 *
 * INDEXES (R6). Blank rows are dropped, but every data-field carries the row's
 * ORIGINAL array index, so deleting card 2 cannot misdirect an edit into card 3.
 * The numeral — decoration — uses the DISPLAY index, so the grid always reads
 * 01, 02, 03 with no gaps.
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5). lib/derive-content-defaults.ts fills services.tag,
 * services.headline AND services.sub on every site (it derives the sub from the
 * business name), so gating on any of those would ship a heading and a paragraph
 * over an empty grid. The gate counts BODY content only: at least one real card.
 * There is no photo or panel in this section to count alongside them.
 *
 * THE ITALIC NUMERAL DECLARES WEIGHT 500 where the design leaves it inherited at
 * 400 — and that is not a re-derivation, because it is what the design already
 * renders. The design and this template load the SAME font link, whose only
 * italic instance is `1,9..144,500`; CSS matches font-style BEFORE font-weight,
 * so an italic span asking for 400 still resolves to that one italic face and is
 * clamped to wght 500. Declaring it says out loud what the design does silently
 * and forecloses a synthesised slant on a fallback face standing beside the h2's
 * genuine italic. The wrapper makes the same call for `.h2 em`. Colour, size and
 * face are the design's, untouched.
 *
 * CONTRAST. --muted on --paper is 3.86:1 — under AA, the design's own value,
 * and PageBL's frontmatter accepts it for 10–13px labels that the heading beside
 * them repeats. Both uses here are that: the 10px card tag and the 12px detail
 * line, each sitting against a card title that names the same thing. Everything
 * that carries meaning of its own clears AA: --ink on --card 15.3 (title,
 * price), --ink-soft on --card 6.71 (blurb, sub), --accent on --card 5.31
 * (eyebrow, numeral).
 *
 * RESPONSIVE (R8). The wrapper owns the shared 760/520 pass; this section owns
 * its own grid. At 900 the three tracks become two, at 620 one — a 24px card
 * heading inside 26px of padding has ~210px of measure at three-across on a
 * 900px viewport, which is narrower than the design ever intended. At 520,
 * checked against a 360px viewport, nothing can push the page sideways: every
 * grid track is minmax(0,1fr), and the sub, title, blurb, tag, detail and price
 * all break anywhere, because a price typed as one long range and a tag typed as
 * one long word are the two likeliest strings on this section to arrive unbroken.
 *
 * SPECIFICITY (R3). PageBL wraps its primitives in :where(), so a scoped rule
 * here at (0,2,0) beats the global at (0,1,1) — including the global's media
 * queries. So: never restate a property the wrapper tunes per breakpoint without
 * restating it at that breakpoint too (that is why .s-sub's max-width and the
 * eyebrow's wrapping reappear below), and never restate a heading's face, weight
 * or colour at all — the wrapper's heading rule already owns them.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

const services = content.services || {};
// `services` is a wrapper OR a bare legacy array, so every scalar read has to
// tolerate both — on an array these are simply undefined, which is what the
// guards below expect.
const tag = services.tag || '';
const headline = services.headline || '';
const sub = services.sub || '';

type Item = { title?: string; desc?: string; tag?: string; note?: string; price?: string };
// Both shapes: the wrapped `{tag, headline, sub, items[]}` and the legacy bare
// array at content.services (see lib/services-shape.ts).
const rawItems: any[] = Array.isArray(services.items) ? services.items : (Array.isArray(services) ? services : []);
// …and all three VOCABULARIES. lib/astro-builder.ts maps name→title /
// description→desc when content.services arrives as a bare array, but NOT when
// it arrives already wrapped — and a wrapped row still carrying the legacy key
// names is exactly what the editor's brief draft-normalisation window wrote.
// Without this mapping every row on such a site fails the "did the owner fill
// anything in?" filter below and the WHOLE MENU vanishes while the data sits
// untouched in the record. A bare string row (the oldest shape of all) becomes
// its own title rather than being spread into character keys and lost.
const items: Item[] = rawItems.map((it: any) => {
  if (typeof it === 'string') return { title: it };
  if (!it || typeof it !== 'object') return {};
  const out: any = { ...it };
  if (out.title == null && out.name != null) out.title = out.name;
  if (out.desc == null && out.description != null) out.desc = out.description;
  return out;
});
// Drop the rows an owner added and never filled — carrying the ORIGINAL index
// (R6). Only the five fields this card DRAWS count; see THE CARD FILTER above.
const cards = items
  .map((it, i: number) => ({ ...it, i }))
  .filter((r) => Boolean(r.title || r.desc || r.tag || r.note || r.price));

// R5: body content only. tag / headline / sub are all derived on every site.
const hasSection = cards.length > 0;
---
{hasSection && (
  <section class="svcs" id="menu">
    <div class="wrap">
      {(tag || headline || sub) && (
        <div class="s-head">
          {(tag || headline) && (
            <div class="s-head-l">
              {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="services.tag">{tag}</span>}
              {headline && <h2 class="h2 s-h2 reveal" data-field="services.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
            </div>
          )}
          {sub && <p class="s-sub" data-field="services.sub">{sub}</p>}
        </div>
      )}

      <div class="s-grid">
        {cards.map((it, k) => (
          <article class="s-card reveal" data-delay={String(k % 3)}>
            <div class="s-top">
              {/* Decoration: the design computes the same numeral from the
                  card's position, it is stored nowhere, and it is announced to
                  nobody. It is the only glyph on this section this file wrote. */}
              <span class="fig s-num" aria-hidden="true">{String(k + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
              {it.tag && <span class="mono s-tag" data-field={`services.items.${it.i}.tag`}>{it.tag}</span>}
            </div>
            {it.title && <h3 class="s-t" data-field={`services.items.${it.i}.title`}>{it.title}</h3>}
            {it.desc && <p class="s-p" data-field={`services.items.${it.i}.desc`}>{it.desc}</p>}
            {/* Guarded OUTSIDE the foot, so a card with neither a detail line
                nor a price draws no hairline across the bottom of nothing. */}
            {(it.note || it.price) && (
              <div class="s-foot">
                {it.note && <span class="s-note" data-field={`services.items.${it.i}.note`}>{it.note}</span>}
                {/* Printed exactly as typed. `margin-left:auto` keeps it on the
                    design's right edge even when the detail line beside it is
                    absent and space-between has nothing to push against. */}
                {it.price && <span class="s-price" data-field={`services.items.${it.i}.price`}>{it.price}</span>}
              </div>
            )}
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* One scoped block. See the SPECIFICITY note in the frontmatter before adding
     to it. (Never write a literal end-style-tag inside a comment here — this is
     a raw-text element and the parser would end the stylesheet on the spot,
     silently truncating the rest of the file.) */

  /* ── the split head: eyebrow + display h2 left, the owner's paragraph right,
        baselines aligned on the bottom edge. Wraps to a stack the moment the
        two cannot share a line. */
  .s-head { display: flex; align-items: flex-end; justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 30px; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-bottom: 36px; }
  .s-head-l { min-width: 0; max-width: 100%; }
  /* Margin and measure only. Size, face, colour, the italic <em> and both mobile
     passes come from the wrapper's .h2 — and the <em> ARRIVES THROUGH set:html,
     so it is unreachable from this scoped block by design (R3). The design gives
     this h2 no measure cap, so the wrapper's 24ch is released. */
  .s-h2 { margin: .26em 0 0; max-width: none; }
  /* The design's sub is 15px/1.75 over 36ch in --ink-soft — smaller and tighter
     than the wrapper's .lede, which is sized for the hero, so this is its own
     rule rather than a .lede override. Its measure is released at 760 below,
     because this base rule out-specifies the wrapper's own pass. Qualified by
     its class so it beats the wrapper's (0,1,1) paragraph reset. */
  .s-sub { margin: 0; max-width: 36ch; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--ink-soft); overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* ── THE CARD GRID. Three equal tracks, 20px gutter. minmax(0,1fr) rather
        than 1fr so a long unbroken price cannot widen its column. */
  .s-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 20px; }
  /* A white plate inside a warm hairline. Column flex so the foot can be pinned
     to the bottom edge and cards in a row stay the same height. */
  .s-card { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 9px; min-width: 0;
    padding: 28px 26px;
    background-color: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r-lg); }

  /* ── the card's top line: numeral left, tag right, one shared baseline. */
  .s-top { display: flex; align-items: baseline; justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 12px; }
  /* .fig supplies the display face, tabular figures and line-height 1; the
     italic, the rust and the size are the design's. Weight 500 is the only real
     Fraunces italic the wrapper loads — see the note in the frontmatter. */
  .s-num { font-size: 19px; font-style: italic; font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--accent); flex: none; }
  /* .mono is the design's tracked caps almost exactly: 10px, uppercase,
     --muted, --sans. The design tracks this one at .18em and leaves it at the
     body's 400, so both are restated. text-align keeps a tag long enough to wrap
     against the right edge it was placed on. */
  .s-tag { min-width: 0; letter-spacing: .18em; font-weight: 400;
    text-align: right; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* Face, weight and colour come from the wrapper's heading rule; only the size,
     the line-height, the margin and the break behaviour are this section's. */
  .s-t { font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.15; margin: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  /* Qualified by its own class so it beats the wrapper's (0,1,1) paragraph
     reset, whose 1em bottom margin would break the card's 9px rhythm. */
  .s-p { margin: 0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.72; color: var(--ink-soft);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* ── the foot. `margin-top:auto` pushes it to the bottom of the plate, so the
        prices line up across a row of cards whose blurbs differ in length. */
  .s-foot { margin-top: auto; padding-top: 16px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line-3);
    display: flex; align-items: baseline; justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 12px; }
  .s-note { min-width: 0; font-size: 12px; color: var(--muted);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  /* Sans, not the serif .fig: the design's price span inherits the body face.
     margin-left:auto holds the right edge when the note is absent. */
  .s-price { min-width: 0; margin-left: auto; text-align: right;
    font-size: 16px; font-weight: 500; color: var(--ink);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* ── 900: three tracks become two. Below this the card's 24px heading has
        less measure inside its 26px padding than the design ever gave it. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .s-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  }

  /* ── 760: the head stacks, and its paragraph gives up the 36ch measure this
        file set (the wrapper's own release cannot reach a (0,2,0) rule). */
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .s-head { align-items: flex-start; gap: 16px; margin-bottom: 28px; }
    .s-sub { max-width: none; }
  }

  /* ── 620: one column. */
  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .s-grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  }

  /* ── 520 (checked against a 360px viewport: nothing here can push the page
        sideways — the grid's one track is minmax(0,1fr) and every owner-typed
        string in the card breaks anywhere). */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .s-card { padding: 24px 20px; }
    .s-num { font-size: 17px; }
    .s-t { font-size: 22px; }
    /* The eyebrow is inline-block from the wrapper, so a single long word an
       owner types would otherwise sit at its full tracked width. */
    .s-head-l .kick { max-width: 100%; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
    .s-tag { letter-spacing: .16em; }
  }
</style>
